From 2ce6cbd9b1635be475ed2f18d59f6a7766676efd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FiyeroT <me@fiyero.xyz>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 12:16:15 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 49/88] xhttp: cap the frame size the HTTP/2 client writes,
 wait for data on a small probe, retire idle pooled connections

A streamed request body was written through a buffer as large as the server's
maximum frame size, up to 512 KiB, for as long as the session lived: every
idle stream-one and stream-up session over HTTP/2 held it (lab: 567 KB per
session against Xray). The first SETTINGS of the server is now read with its
SETTINGS_MAX_FRAME_SIZE at 16 KiB, the initial value, what Chrome sends.

The copy loop waits for download data through a read waiter that reads a
512 byte probe, instead of holding its 32 KiB buffer while the server is
silent.

A pooled connection no session used for five minutes is retired: QUIC's
keep-alive kept an idle HTTP/3 connection open for good.
